data_pocket_with_ligand
#
# Hand-written for tests/unit/test_pocket.py, following this project's own
# fixture discipline (CLAUDE.md, 2026-08-13 "DNA renders, and the ramp says
# what it means"): the pocket/non-pocket answer must be settled by real 3D
# position, never by a construction that would look the same whichever
# residue a bug picked.
#
# Chain A, protein (CA-only, enough for gemmi to classify via
# gemmi.find_tabulated_residue -- no ribbon is built from this fixture, so
# no other backbone atom is needed):
#   ALA 1  x=0    -- 1.0 A from the ligand atom below. INSIDE every cutoff
#                    this test file uses.
#   GLY 2  x=10   -- 9.0 A from the ligand. Inside a 15 A cutoff, outside a
#                    5 A one -- this is what test_widening_the_cutoff_
#                    catches_more actually exercises.
#   LEU 3  x=100  -- 99.0 A from the ligand. Outside every cutoff used here,
#                    so a bug that always includes "the last residue" or
#                    "every residue" is caught by its mere presence in the
#                    file.
#   ASP 4  x=6.0  -- EXACTLY 5.0 A from the ligand. Pins the "at least one
#                    atom within cutoff_angstrom" boundary as inclusive
#                    (<=): a `<` implementation excludes this residue at the
#                    default 5.0 A cutoff, and no other residue in this file
#                    sits exactly on a cutoff used by any test, so nothing
#                    else would catch that off-by-one.
#
# Chain C, one DNA nucleotide (DA, x=1.5) sitting only 0.5 A from the ligand
# -- closer than every protein residue above -- specifically so a bug that
# forgets `pocket_residues` promises PROTEIN residues (counting any
# near polymer atom regardless of chemistry) is caught: it would add
# ("C", 1) to every result below, and no test tolerates that.
#
# Chain B, one ligand atom (HETATM, residue LIG) at x=1. Real methotrexate
# geometry is exercised in tests/unit/test_ligand.py; this fixture only
# needs `ui.ligand.ligands_from_structure` to recognize LIG as a
# non-polymer, which it does the same way for any unrecognized 3-letter
# code (see tests/unit/test_pocket.py's own comment).
#
loop_
_atom_site.group_PDB
